<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Every household runs on about thirty documents it can never find: the
    lease, the policy, the warranty, the letter from the council, the thing
    from the hospital. They're in a drawer, an email, a photo on somebody's
    phone, or a folder called Scans.
  </p>
  <p>
    Filing them is the boring half. The half that matters is being able to
    answer a question about one at the moment the question arrives.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What happens when you add one</h2>
  <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">1 · The text comes out</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Including from a photograph of a letter. That's what makes the whole
        vault searchable by a word you half-remember, rather than by
        whatever you named the file.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">2 · It gets summarised</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        A plain-language account of what the document actually says, so you
        don't have to reread eleven pages of policy to remember whether it
        covers the boiler.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">3 · It gets filed where it belongs</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Into the parts of your life it relates to — often more than one. You
        can correct it, and it stays corrected.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">4 · The rest of the app can use it</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        This is the point. When you appeal a denial, the appeal reads the
        denial. When you demand a deposit, the letter reads the lease. You
        filed it once and it works for you afterwards.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Without a model</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    You still get the vault: files kept safely, text extracted, full-text
    search, your own filing. It's the summaries and the automatic filing
    that need a model, and the app says so rather than quietly doing
    nothing.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Where they live</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    On your disk, in a folder you can copy. If you'd rather keep the bulk
    somewhere else — a storage service you rent, a NAS in the cupboard —
    the app can be pointed there instead, and nothing about how it works
    changes.
  </p>
</section>
